 "productionNotes": "Lamborghini's own closing figure is 4,099 cars. Autoblog, reproducing the factory press release, reports that 4,099 units were built between 2001 and 2010 at Sant'Agata Bolognese, and Motor Authority repeats 4,099 as the number on the last car. That figure is not broken down by body, by year or by market in either source. Wikipedia carries the same 4,099 in its introduction but prints a year-by-year table that adds to 3,983 (3,084 coupes and 899 Roadsters), 116 cars short. Nothing fetched for this page explains the 116-car gap, so no total is entered. The 2001 row (65 cars) matters to a US reader because Wikipedia states the car was first available in North America for the 2002 model year; none of the year tables says how many cars of any year came to the US. Variant counts disagree too. The LP670-4 SuperVeloce was announced at 350 cars (Gardner Business Media, Edmunds, Wikipedia). Sports Car Market, profiling a car sold at Bonhams' Goodwood Revival auction, says Lamborghini built 186 and could sell only that many, while a summary line on the same page says 185. Wikipedia says an owner-run registry had found 268 as of 2025, which is more than the 186 built. The LP650-4 Roadster was a run of 50 (Edmunds). Wikipedia lists the Reventon at 21 coupes (one kept by the factory) and 15 Roadsters. No source consulted gives a US-only count for any variant. The nearest thing is Cars.com's 2002 launch article, which says fifteen US dealers would receive about 35 percent of an expected 140 cars, an allocation rather than a delivery count. A US list price for the 2003 through 2006 and 2009 model years was not found in a fetched source, and the gap is stated rather than filled.",
